ue as £110,185, creating unexplained shortfall of £6,813.47","Employment income qualifier: page 3 describes income as 'typically earning around £300 per week net' suggesting variability, but page 14 cashflow assumptions section lists 'You receive £300 per week as an HGV driver' without the qualifier 'typically', treating it as deterministic input","Risk rating sequence ambiguity: page 4 contains two statements—'We agreed that you are a risk rating 5 rather than a 4 as per the questionnaire' and later 'You confirmed that you wish to increase your Risk rating to 5'—creating unclear causal sequence between questionnaire result (4), adviser assessment/agreement, and client confirmation","Fund name consistency: page 2 recommendation summary lists 'CT Universal Map Balanced' and 'HSBC Global Strategy Balanced Portfolio' while page 12 lists 'CT Universal MAP' and 'HSBC' with allocations 50% each; the fuller names on page 2 appear to be the same funds but naming inconsistency exists across pages","Retention vs consolidation logic: recommendation states 'Retain your people's pension plan - £6,601.55' but objective OBJ3 directs consolidation of pensions; the retention of this one plan while consolidating/switching four others is not reconciled"],"comparisonPerformed":true,"missingInputs":[],"analysis":"All six identified discrepancies are internal contradictions within the report itself and can be assessed without external evidence. Severity assessment: (1) The Aviva charge contradiction is material—page 6 asserts the new plan is 0.06% more expensive than recommended solution, but the fee table on page 11 and the arithmetic show the opposite (0.44% new vs 0.50% old = 0.06% cheaper). This is a high-severity self-contradiction on a figure that could drive the recommendation. (2) The transfer value shortfall of £6,813.47 is material and unexplained; it appears the report may have excluded one or more arrangements from the total without stating why, or there is an arithmetic error. This is high severity as transfer value is a driver of the recommendation. (3) The employment income qualifier ('typically' vs deterministic) is a moderate concern; the cashflow model treats £300/week as fixed but the narrative suggests it varies, which affects reliability of the financial projection. (4) The risk rating sequence is ambiguous but does not constitute a numerical contradiction—both statements conclude to risk rating 5, so the final position is clear even if the path to it is unclear; this is low-to-moderate concern affecting the documentation of consent. (5) Fund naming inconsistency (Universal Map vs MAP; full name vs abbreviation) appears to refer to the same funds and is a low-severity presentation issue. (6) The retention of People's Pension contradicts the stated consolidation objective, but the extraction notes this is a logical/strategic inconsistency rather than a data mismatch on the figure itself—still material and moderate severity. The core numerical contradictions are the Aviva charge statement (high severity) and the transfer value shortfall (high severity).","citations":[],"severity":"High","outcome":"PotentialConcern"}

