## Accessing your pension savings
## Visit www.thepeoplespension.co.uk/ minimum-pension-age-change
to find out the earliest age you can access your pension savings. You don't have to stop working to take your pension pot.
You can access your pension savings at any age on medical grounds if you're unable to continue your usual occupation, and as a result you've stopped working. If you're suffering from serious ill health (with a life expectancy of less than 12 months), it may be possible for you to receive all the savings in your pension pot tax free as a serious ill-health lump sum.
## Ways you can take your money
The options available to you with The People's Pension depend on how much is in your pension pot when you choose to access it.
If your pension pot is worth £10,000 or less when you access it, you may be able to take your money all in one go as a 'small pot lump sum'.
With occupational pension pots (like The People's Pension), you can take as many small pot lump sums as you want. The first 25% is usually tax free, with the other 75% being subject to tax. Depending on the size of your pot and your tax status, parts of it may be subject to higher rates of tax.
Before  you're  able  to  take  your  money,  all  contributions into your pension pot must have stopped. You must also meet all HMRC's rules about when a small pot lump sum can be taken.
If your pension pot is worth more than £10,000 when you access it, you can either take your money as a lump sum, or take your money a bit at a time in one of two ways.
The first option is through 'flexi access drawdown' (FAD). You take your tax-free cash up front (usually up to 25%) either a bit at a time or in one lump sum. For every £1 you take as tax-free cash, £3 will be moved into a flexi-access drawdown account. Then each time you take money out of the flexi-access drawdown account, you may pay tax on the full amount of each lump sum. It's added to your taxable income and, depending on your tax status, parts of it may be liable for higher rates of tax.
