 'Lower Costs — The receiving scheme has a lower overall cost structure compared to your existing pension' (page 7, P21) but the computed charge figures show the recommended solution's total ongoing charge of 0.44% is not demonstrably lower than all existing arrangements. The existing Aviva arrangement (EA3) is 0.50% (page 5 per canonical model), the existing Zurich (EA4) is 0.93%, but the Standard Life arrangement (EA5) is stated as 0.18% on page 5, and the Zurich Managed Pn AP (EA4) fund charge is 0.18% plus 0.10% platform = 0.28% or 0.93% depending on interpretation (canonical ambiguity at /existingArrangements/3/chargesPercentage). The claim of lower cost is therefore inconsistent with the evidence that at least one existing arrangement (Standard Life at 0.18%) is substantially lower than the recommended 0.44%.","The report claims Enhanced Retirement Flexibility as a benefit (page 7, P21) justifying the recommendation, but the existing Zurich plan already offers full and partial UFPLS (P5, P9) and existing Standard Life offers FAD (P5), so this flexibility is not new and cannot justify additional cost under the guard that 'a benefit the existing plan already provides cannot justify the extra cost'.","The report claims 'Greater Investment Choice' as a benefit (page 7, P21), noting Zurich does not offer risk level 5 funds (page 6, P21), but the evidence shows Zurich offers 60+ funds (P5, P19) whereas Standard Life offers 300 funds (P5, P19) and Aviva offers 80+ funds (P5, P19). The existing arrangements already provide substantial choice, making this a marginal rather than material differentiator.","The report claims lifeystyling risk as a benefit justifying the switch (page 7, P21: 'Your funds are currently invested in lifestyling funds, which automatically reduce investment risk as you approach retirement. As a result, your portfolio will eventually move outside of your agreed risk range'). However, the evidence shows lifestyling is present in existing Aviva (P7) and Scottish Widows (P7) arrangements, and the recommendation to consolidate all five pensions into one Aviva plan (page 6, P20) perpetuates this arrangement — the recommended funds are HSBC Global Strategy Balanced Portfolio and CT Universal MAP Balanced (both risk 5, page 12, P22), which do not mitigate the lifestyling risk that triggered the recommendation; instead, they replace lifestyling with static risk-rated funds, but this addresses the client's risk alignment rather than justifying cost.","The report's claim of 'Ongoing Monitoring and Adjustments' (page 7, P21) supported by an ongoing adviser charging facilitation benefit appears without evidence in the case file of the agreed adviser fee structure, service level, frequency of monitoring, or correspondence with a client engagement letter. The canonical model notes adviser fees are extracted but no adviser remuneration evidence is provided to corroborate this service proposition is unique or justifies the cost difference.","The report asserts on page 6 (P21) that 'the plan itself is 0.06% more expensive than the recommended solution' but the charge comparison table on page 11 (P22 shows Zurich at 0.50% existing vs. the recommended solution at approximately 0.44% (0.21% + 0.22%), implying the new plan is 0.06% cheaper, contradicting the text claim. This internal inconsistency undermines the cost justification narrative."],"comparisonPerformed":true,"missingInputs":["Adviser fee structure and client agreement showing the agreed ongoing adviser charges and frequency of monitoring to support the 'Ongoing Monitoring and Adjustments' and 'Adviser Charging Facilitation' benefits claimed on page 7 (P21).","Documentation confirming whether the recommended funds (HSBC and CT Universal MAP, both risk 5) address the lifestyling risk concern or merely replace one static allocation with another, and how this justifies the cost given that lifestyling is also present in existing Aviva and Scottish Widows arrangements.","A cost comparison table aggregating all charges (platform, investment management, fund charges, adviser fees, transaction costs) across existing arrangements and the recommended solution to support the 'Lower Costs' claim, as the canonical charge arithmetic shows inconsistent fund values across different charge layers.","Evidence of the specific benefits the client will receive from 'Adviser Charging Facilitation' and whether existing arrangements precluded this or charged differently, to distinguish between a new benefit and a procedural convenience."],"analysis":"The report identifies eight benefits to support the recommendation, but several fail the consistency test against evidence and the guards. First, 'Enhanced Retirement Flexibility' is already present in existing arrangements (Zurich offers full/partial UFPLS, Standard Life offers FAD per P5 and P9), so under the guard that a benefit the existing plan already provides cannot justify extra cost, this cannot be relied upon. Second, 'Greater Investment Choice' is marginal: Standard Life (300 funds) exceeds Aviva (80+ funds) substantially, so choice per se is not a material differentiator justifying cost. Third, the lifestyling risk benefit is internally inconsistent: the report cites lifestyling as a reason to switch, but the recommended funds are static risk-rated instruments, not a solution to the lifestyling problem — they replace one approach with another, and the same client is being moved to Aviva, which the evidence shows (P7) already employs lifestyling in the existing Aviva pension my future focus consolidation. The benefit claimed is addressing risk drift, not cost-justifying the switch. Fourth, the 'Lower Costs' claim is contradicted by arithmetic: the recommended solution is stated at 0.44% ongoing (page 11, P22: 0.21% platform + 0.22% investment management), but this is not lower than the existing Standard Life arrangement at 0.18% (canonical model, page 5), and the text on page 6 (P21) contradicts itself by stating 'the plan itself is 0.06% more expensive' when the comparison table implies it is cheaper. This internal contradiction and the fact that at least one existing arrangement is substantially lower undermines the lower-cost justification. Fifth, 'Ongoing Monitoring and Adjustments' and 'Adviser Charging Facilitation' are claimed without supporting evidence of the adviser agreement, fee structure, or specific services that differentiate this from existing arrangements. The report does not evidence that existing advisers were not providing similar monitoring, or that the new structure delivers measurably more value. Sixth, the cost justification based on 'past performance' and critical yield (pages 11, 13, P21) relies on the assertion that recommended funds will 'outperform by 4.8% per year' to justify the 0.44% annual charge, but this is not supported by documented performance comparison between existing and recommended funds in the evidence, nor is the critical yield calculation shown. The guards state that a benefit the client will never use cannot support cost, and consolidation convenience is real but not financial; here, consolidation and monitoring benefits are presented as cost-justifiers without quantifying the financial impact or proving the client values them over lower-cost alternatives. The report's articulation of benefits reads as general value propositions for Aviva rather than client-specific, cost-justified assertions grounded in evidence. The discrepancy between the claim of lower costs and the arithmetic showing the existing Standard Life at 0.18% is lower than the recommended 0.44%, combined with the internal text contradiction on page 6, means the core benefit claim—lower costs—is not supported by and is contradicted by the evidence provided.","citations":[{"passageId":"P21","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":"Lower Costs - The receiving scheme has a lower overall cost structure compared to your existing pension, meaning more of your savings remain invested for growth rather than being lost to charges.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P5","source":"Test Case 1 - Policy Research -2025.md","category":"G","quote":null,"cells":["Standard Life","0.18%","300 funds"]},{"passageId":"P22","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":"CT Universal MAP Balanced C | 50% | 5 | 0.29%","cells":null},{"passageId":"P21","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":"Enhanced Retirement Flexibility - The receiving scheme provides more flexible retirement options, ensuring you can access your pension savings in a way that best suits your needs, including tax-efficient withdrawal strategies.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P5","source":"Test Case 1 - Policy Research -2025.md","category":"G","quote":null,"cells":["Zurich","Full and partial UFPLS","60+ funds"]},{"passageId":"P5","source":"Test Case 1 - Policy Research -2025.md","category":"G","quote":null,"cells":["Standard Life","FAD","300 funds"]},{"passageId":"P7","source":"Test Case 1 - Policy Research -2025.md","category":"G","quote":null,"cells":["Aviva Pensions My Future Focus Consolidation","Yes","Invested in lifestyle strategy"]},{"passageId":"P7","source":"Test Case 1 - Policy Research -2025.md","category":"G","quote":null,"cells":["SW Pension Portfolio Four CS1 & Three CS1","Yes","Invested in lifestyle strategy"]},{"passageId":"P21","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":"the plan itself is 0.06% more expensive than the recommended solution","cells":null},{"passageId":"P22","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":null,"cells":["HSBC Global Strategy Balanced Portfolio C Acc","50%","5","0.18%"]},{"passageId":"P10","source":"SIPP research report.md","category":"G","quote":null,"cells":["HSBC Global Strategy Balanced Portfolio Accumulation C","-","0.18%","0.01%","0.19%"]},{"passageId":"P10","source":"SIPP research report.md","category":"G","quote":null,"c