{"groupId":"CHK-008","requirement":"[G8.3] Tax consequences of the recommendation — Consistency","reportSays":"The suitability report states five tax-related consequences: (1) a tax treatment caveat on page 16 stating 'All statements concerning the tax treatment of products and their benefits are based on our understanding of current tax law and HMRC practice. Levels and basis of tax relief are subject to change.'; (2) on page 9, that the client 'understand[s] the pension freedoms and prefer[s] the flexibility of entering Flexi Access Drawdown when you access your pension'; (3) on page 9, death benefit taxation: 'Should you die before age 75, the nominated beneficiaries can receive a lump sum, drawdown an income or purchase a dependents annuity. All these options are paid free of tax. Where you die after the age of 75, the same options are available, but tax is paid at the beneficiary's marginal rate of tax.'; (4) on page 9, tax relief on contributions: 'Personal contributions to the pension benefit from valuable tax relief; for example, a net contribution of £80 effectively becomes £100 gross in your pension.'; (5) on page 6, small pots taxation: 'Under the small pot rules, a £200 pension fund withdrawal would be taxed with 25% taken as tax-free cash and the remaining 75% taxed as income at your marginal tax rate. Estimated net receipt is £170.' The small pots implication is stated as a recommendation tax implication specific to the Scottish Widows encashment.","fileSays":"The evidence corroborates the tax treatment statements. Key Features (P2, P4) confirm: (1) withdrawal of up to 25% as tax-free lump sum with income tax on remainder treated as income; (2) HMRC limits on lump sum allowance (£268,275) and lump sum & death benefit allowance (£1,073,100); (3) on death before 75, benefits normally paid tax-free to beneficiaries unless lump sum & death benefit allowance exceeded (P4); (4) on death after 75, benefits taxed at beneficiary's marginal rate (P4); (5) tax relief on contributions (P6 confirms 'Personal contributions to the pension benefit from valuable tax relief'); (6) Flexi-access drawdown allows up to 25% as tax-free lump sum with remainder withdrawn at marginal rate (P6). The Aviva policy info (P29) confirms automatic movement to annuity at age 75 if no drawdown election made. The small pots calculation of £170 net from £200 is based on 25% tax-free (£50) and 75% taxable (£150) at marginal rate. At a 20% basic rate, £150 × 0.8 = £120, yielding £170 net, which is arithmetically correct for a basic rate taxpayer.","discrepancies":["The report states the client will receive £170 net from a £200 small pots withdrawal, implying a 20% tax rate on the 75% taxable portion (£150 × 0.8 = £120, plus £50 tax-free = £170). However, the client's marginal tax rate is not established in the evidence pack. The report describes the client as earning typically £300 per week net (P5) and assumes state pension income of £230 per week, totalling roughly £15,600 net annually plus spouse income of £13,200, with household expenditure of £20,400 per annum (P5, P27). The net income figures and spending profile do not directly establish the client's income tax band or marginal rate. Without confirmation of the client's actual tax position, the £170 estimate cannot be verified as correctly applying the client's marginal rate.","No other substantive discrepancies identified. The death benefit taxation (before/after age 75), Flexi Access Drawdown mechanics, tax relief on contributions, and the tax treatment caveat are all supported by the Key Features and product literature."],"comparisonPerformed":false,"missingInputs":["Client's confirmed marginal income tax rate or tax band for the current and retirement years","Confirmation that the client is a basic rate (20%) taxpayer, necessary to validate the £170 net receipt calculation from the £200 small pots withdrawal"],"analysis":"The report asserts five tax consequences, of which four are generic legislative explanations supported by product literature: death benefits taxation, Flexi Access Drawdown mechanics, tax relief on contributions, and the tax treatment caveat. These are not client-specific consequences and require no further validation beyond consistency with product terms, which they have. However, the small pots tax consequence is client-specific: the report estimates '£170 net' receipt from a £200 withdrawal under small pots rules. This calculation depends on the client's actual marginal rate of income tax. The working appears to assume 20% (basic rate): £50 tax-free + (£150 × 0.8) = £170. The evidence confirms the client is aged 69, currently earning £300/week net as an HGV driver (P5), receives £230/week state pension (P5), and has a spouse earning £1,100/month net (P5), with household expenditure of £1,700/month (P5). These gross income figures are not stated, and the client's personal marginal tax rate is not established anywhere in the file. The Fact Find (P40) contains sections for tax band but no entry is visible. Without the client's established tax rate, the £170 figure cannot be verified as a correct application of the consequence to this client's circumstances. Under COBS 2.1.1R and the false-positive guards, a tax consequence stated without the client's tax band established is unverifiable. The guard acknowledges that a tax consequence stated generically is not necessarily wrong, but where it is stated as a client-specific quantified outcome (£170), the underlying tax assumption must be disclosed or confirmed. The report does not state or justify the 20% assumption. This is a material omission where the recommendation depends on the tax treatment of the withdrawal.","citations":[{"passageId":"P2","source":"Key Features (1).md","category":"H","quote":"You can normally withdraw up to 25% of your pension fund as a tax-free lump sum. You may have to pay income tax on any income payments and lump sum payments you take. Both are treated as income, and therefore the tax you pay will depend on your personal circumstances.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P4","source":"Key Features (1).md","category":"H","quote":"If you die before you're 75, we'll normally pay any benefits tax free to your nominated beneficiaries, at the trustees' discretion (unless you have an Aviva Pension Trust, in which case the rules of that Trust will apply). If the value of tax-free benefits taken from your pension plan(s) during your lifetime and on death is more than the lump sum & death benefit allowance, the beneficiary may pay income tax on the excess.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P4","source":"Key Features (1).md","category":"H","quote":"If you die after the age of 75 we can pay the full value of your remaining pension fund to your nominated beneficiaries, at the discretion of the trustees. Any benefits will be taxed at your beneficiaries' marginal rate.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P6","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":"Flexi-access drawdown provides a flexible and tax-efficient means of drawing benefits from a pension fund. An individual can take up to 25% of the pension fund being crystallised as a tax-free lump sum at outset (subject to the Lump Sum Allowance of £268,275 for people with no form of lifetime allowance protection). The remainder of the fund remains invested within a tax advantaged environment from which the individual can take regular or ad-hoc income withdrawals to suit their needs and circumstances. There is no upper limit to the amount the individual can withdraw as an income. However, each withdrawal is taxed at their marginal rate of Income Tax.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P5","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":"You are currently aged 69 and married to Kim, aged 65. You are both in good health and have three grown-up children, all of whom are financially independent. You work full-time as an HGV driver through an agency, typically earning around £300 