{"groupId":"CHK-009","requirement":"[G9.7] Other alternatives provide better outcomes for the client — Limb: Appropriateness. Where the Suitability Report contains a recommendation to replace, switch, transfer, consolidate, encash or surrender an existing product or investment, alternatives considered must be evidenced and any that provide better outcomes must be addressed in the analysis.","reportSays":"The report considers five main options: (1) Leave existing pension untouched; (2) Leave existing pension and switch investment funds; (3) Switch into another existing pension arrangement; (4) Switch into employer's pension arrangement; (5) Set up new pension plan (recommended). For the employer scheme (People's Pension), the report rejects internal fund switches citing no multi-asset funds and limited options, and states funds do not meet risk profile. For Zurich, it rejects internal switch because Zurich offers no risk level 5 funds and blended approach reduces diversification, plus blended funds have not performed as well as Aviva options. For Scottish Widows, the report presents both encashment and retention as options then recommends encashment. The report states the main priority is consolidation, control, and drawdown flexibility. For Standard Life, it rejects retention due to lifestyling funds moving outside agreed risk range and recommends switch. The report recommends switching four plans (Scottish Widows encash, Aviva switch, Zurich switch, Standard Life switch) while retaining People's Pension workplace scheme due to ongoing employer contributions.","fileSays":"The pension switch report (P1-P8, P11-P14) contains projections comparing existing arrangements against new solution. For the overall position, it shows existing pensions projected value of £141,000 versus new solution £138,000 (P1), a reduction of £3,000 or 2.1% at mid-growth rate. For Zurich alone (P3), existing £102,000 vs new solution £100,000 at low growth (0.0%), with critical yield of 0.3% required. At mid-growth 2.94%, Zurich existing £127,000 vs new £125,000, critical yield 3.2%, effect of switch £2,000 or 1.6% decrease. For Aviva existing alone (P5), at mid-growth, existing £4,280 vs new solution £4,260, critical yield 2.9%, effect of switch £20 or 0.5% decrease. For Standard Life (P8), at mid-growth, existing £127,000 vs new £125,000, critical yield 3.2%, effect of switch £2,000 or 1.6% decrease. Provider information (P9, P20, P21, P22) confirms Zurich has no guarantees or safeguarded benefits listed. The suitability report (P10) states for Zurich: 'blended funds have not performed as well as the options available through Aviva, albeit marginally' and recommends switch for consolidation and drawdown flexibility. For Standard Life (P17), report states 'The plan itself is 0.08% more expensive than the recommended solution' and lifestyling funds move outside agreed risk range. Policy information shows People's Pension is current workplace scheme with ongoing employer contributions (P19, P22-23).","discrepancies":["The report claims for Zurich that 'the blended funds have not performed as well as the options available through Aviva, albeit marginally' (P10) yet the switch comparison table (P3) shows at mid-growth rate 2.94%, Zurich existing yields £127,000 while new solution yields only £125,000, a reduction of £2,000 or 1.6%. This contradicts the narrative claim of better performance in Aviva options.","The report recommends switching Zurich citing that blended approach 'reduces diversification' and recommends multi-asset funds (P10), but the proposed solution (P4) invests 50% CT Universal MAP Balanced and 50% HSBC Global Strategy Balanced — both are balanced/diversified multi-asset funds, not a different diversification profile than the Zurich blended approach described.","For Standard Life, the report states (P17) 'The plan itself is 0.08% more expensive than the recommended solution' but no evidence is provided in the pension switch report (P1-P14, P16) showing a Standard Life-specific switch analysis. The cost comparison cannot be verified from the file.","The report prioritizes consolidation as a primary objective (P10: 'your main priority is to consolidate your pensions') yet People's Pension is retained unchanged, contradicting the stated consolidation objective. The report does not explain why consolidation applies to four schemes but not to the workplace scheme.","For People's Pension, the report rejects internal fund switch citing 'no multi-asset funds available within the plan and limited range of other options' (P10) but provides no evidence (policy document, fund list, or comparison) confirming People's Pension lacks multi-asset funds or has a limited range compared to the new solution.","For Scottish Widows, the report presents encashment as a considered alternative (canonical model states 'Leave pension invested and allow to grow' was inferred as considered with no rejection reasons, then recommends encashment) but does not disclose the tax treatment, regulatory conditions, or why encashment is better than retaining a small pension (£211.32)."],"comparisonPerformed":false,"missingInputs":["Standard Life-specific switch analysis table with cost and benefit comparison — the report asserts cost difference of 0.08% but no switch comparison table is provided in the evidence.","People's Pension fund list and multi-asset fund availability confirmation — the report rejects internal switch citing no multi-asset funds but no plan documentation is evidenced.","Tax and regulatory analysis for Scottish Widows encashment — the report recommends encashment of small pension but provides no disclosure of tax treatment or small pots rules compliance.","Performance comparison detail for Zurich blended funds vs Aviva multi-asset funds — the report claims Aviva performs better 'albeit marginally' but the switch table shows existing Zurich value is higher than new solution.","Employer contribution analysis for People's Pension — while retention is justified by ongoing contributions, no quantification of the value of contributions is provided to support that retention is more beneficial than switching."],"analysis":"A full comparison cannot be performed because critical evidence is absent. The report asserts alternatives were considered but key alternatives lack supporting analysis in the retrieved documents. Most significantly: (1) For Zurich, the report's narrative claim that Aviva performs better contradicts the switch projection table, which shows the existing Zurich arrangement yields a higher maturity value (£127,000 vs £125,000 at mid-growth), yet the report still recommends switching. This is not explained. (2) For Standard Life, the report claims a cost saving of 0.08% but no Standard Life-specific switch comparison is provided in the pension switch report; the cost claim is unsubstantiated. (3) For People's Pension, the report rejects an in-situ fund switch on grounds of no multi-asset funds, but no plan documentation confirms this limitation, and the stated objective of consolidation is not explained as inapplicable to this plan. (4) The overall position projection (P1) shows existing pensions at £141,000 vs new solution at £138,000, a net loss of £3,000 or 2.1% — yet the recommendation proceeds without addressing why switching results in a lower projected value. (5) For Scottish Widows, encashment is recommended without explanation of tax treatment, regulatory small pots conditions, or comparison to alternative retention. The report does reference consolidation and drawdown flexibility as drivers, but these are not quantified against the projected value loss shown in the switch report. The extracted data notes an inconsistency: transfer value stated as £110,185 but individual arrangements total £116,998.47, a shortfall of £6,813.47, which may affect whether comparisons are reliable. The guard on consolidation is noted: if CHK-002 confirms consolidation was a client-stated objective, then consolidation-as-driver is valid; however, the application is inconsistent (four plans consolidated, one retained with no explanation beyond employer contributions, which are not quantified). The performance comparison for Zurich fails the materiality test: the projection shows existing Zurich performs better, making the recommendation harder to justify on performance grounds alone, yet the narrative emphasizes performance. This is a material discrepancy between what the report asserts and what the projections show.","citations":[{"passageId":"P1","source":"Pension switch report - updated.md","category":"G","quote":"Projected value at age 75: Existing pension £141,000, New solution £138,000. Effect of switching: -£3,000 or 2.1% decrease","cells":null},{"passageId":"P3","source":"Pension switch report - updated.md","category":"G","quote":"Zurich - Zurich pension only. Mid growth rate (2.94%). Projected value at age 75: Existing pension £127,000, New solution £125,000. Effect of switching: -£2,000 or 1.6% decrease","cells":null},{"passageId":"P10","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":"the blended funds have not performed as well as the options available through Aviva, albeit marginally","cells":null},{"passageId":"P10","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":"your main priority is to consolidate your pensions, gain better control over them, and be well-positioned for retirement with all available drawdown options","cells":null},{"passageId":"P10","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":"There are no Multi-Asset funds and only a limited range of other options.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P17","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":"The plan itself is 0.08% more expensive than the recommended solution","cells":null},{"passageId":"P19","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":"This is your current workplace scheme, and you and your em